title: Algorithm – List Processing

files:
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/ListBench.em.ts
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/CoreBench.em.ts
  - .assets/img-102-05-01.png
  - .assets/img-102-05-02.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - view 3
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;%[cu]ListBench% Algorithm}

      The last **CoreMark** algorithm features typical *list process&shy;ing* operations, and plays a special role in the benchmark.

      The {[cu]ListBench} module tracks `core_list_join.c`, while also implementing the common {[cu]BenchAlgI} interface.

      Unlike its sibling modules, the {[cu]CoreBench} um&shy;brella *only* calls {[cu]ListBench}.{[cf]run} when executing the benchmark.

      Recall that {[cu]CoreBench} also bound {[cu]ListBench}.{[cn]Board&lbrack;01&rbrack;} proxies to {[cu]StateBench} and {[cu]MatrixBench} at build-time.

      {[in]Take a quick peek at the %[cx]CoreBench.em.ts% editor tab.}

      Before examining its more complex codebase, we'll first introduce some key internal elements of {[cu]ListBench}.

  - cmds:
      - close 2
      - close 3
      - view 4
    text: |
      {[ht,$peek]&thinsp;%[cu]ListBench% Codescape}

      {[dc,1]} This module encapsulates a singly-linked list containing {[ct]Elem} and {[ct]Data} nodes; the latter type holds two numbers.

      {[dc,2]} {[cu]ListBench}.{[cf]setup} seeds each {[ct]Data} node with two numbers and points {[cn]cur_head} at the list's first {[ct]Elem}.

      {[dc,3]} {[cu]ListBench}.{[cf]run} uses helpers to re-arrange the list.

      {[dc,4]} The {[cf]find} helper locates {[ct]Data} nodes by {[cn]idx} or {[cn]val}.

      {[dc,5]} A generic {[cf]sort} function abstracts list ordering by re&shy;ceiving a callback conforming to the {[ct]Comparator} type.

      {[dc,6]} The {[cf]idxCompare}{[ct]Comparator} sorts by {[ct]Data}.{[cn]idx}.

      {[dc,7]} Using <i>other</i>&thinsp;{[ct]BenchI} modules, {[cf]valCompare} sorts the list using a calculated value derived from each {[ct]Data} node.

  - cmds:
      - close 2
      - view 4
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 8
      - mark 1 B 10
      - mark 1 C 13
      - mark 1 D 18
      - mark 1 E 20
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]ListBench% &ndash; Static Declarations}

      Like its siblings, {[cu]ListBench} exports a {[cn]mem_size} config {[bm,A]} assigned by {[cu]CoreBench} at build-time.

      {[cu]CoreBench} also binds {[cn]Bench&lbrack;01&rbrack;} proxies declared at {[bm,B]}, which in fact will support a {[cu]ListBench}.{[cf]sort} helper.

      The prior tour stop introduced the list-node types {[ct]Data}{[bm,C]}and {[ct]Elem}{[bm,E]}, plus a {[ct]Comparator}{[bm,D]} type used by {[cf]sort}.

      {[in]For brevity, we've taken syntactic liberties when defining types %[bm,C]%&middot;%[bm,D]%&middot;%[bm,E]% in our earlier codescape diagram.}

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 8
      - mark 1 B 10
      - mark 1 C 13
      - mark 1 D 18
      - mark 1 E 20
      - mark 1 F 34
      - mark 1 G 37
      - mark 1 H 48
    focus: [1, 24]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]ListBench% &ndash; %[cx]em$meta% Initialization}

      The {[cs]em$construct} function creates the linked data struc&shy;ture shown as item {[dc,2]}in the prior codescape figure.

      After anchoring the first {[ct]Elem} at {[bm,F]}, {[cu]ListBench} calcu&shy;lates {[cn]max_elems} at {[bm,G]} based on its memory budget.

      The {[ck]for} loop uses the **`$$add`** intrinsic to al&shy;locate {[ct]Elem} and {[ct]Data} nodes before linking them together into the list.

      {[in]Note that the %[cn]idx% and %[cn]val% fields of each %[ct]Data% node remain unassigned at this time.}

      {[cu]ListBench} will then initialize {[cn]cur_head} {[bm,H]} at *run-time*, via the {[cn]cur_head_c} config bound here {[bm,F]} at *build-time*.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 108
      - mark 1 B 109
      - mark 1 C 129
    focus: [1, 111]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]ListBench% &ndash; %[cf]setup% Details}

      As noted previously, {[cu]ListBench}.{[cf]setup} initializes {[bm,A]} its {[cn]cur_head} variable with the frozen value of {[cn]cur_head_c}.

      Like other {[cf]setup} functions, {[cu]ListBench} uses a run-time {[ct]seed_t} value {[bm,B]} to randomly initialize its {[ct]Data} nodes.

      As a final step {[bm,C]}, {[cf]setup} sorts the list in {[ct]Data}.{[cn]idx} order before updating the {[cn]cur_head} variable.

      {[in]Suffice it to say, we now have a linked-list similar to codescape item %[dc,2]% &ndash; ready for run-time manipulation.}

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 62
      - mark 1 B 66
      - mark 1 C 69
      - mark 1 D 71
      - mark 1 E 72
      - mark 1 F 90
    focus: [1, 64]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]ListBench% &ndash; %[cf]run% Details (Search Phase)}

      {[bm,A]} {[cu]ListBench}.{[cf]run} uses its local {[cn]retval} variable to re&shy;cord a {[ct]sum_t} value eventually returned by this function.

      {[bm,B]} The {[cf]run} function also prepares a local variable of type {[ct]Data}, used in subsequent searches.

      {[bm,C]} Note how {[cu]ListBench} seeds this {[ck]for} loop's {[cn]find_cnt} termination value at run-time.

      {[bm,D]} Recall the two forms of {[cf]find} [codescape {[dc,4]}], which can match the current {[cn]idx} *or* {[cn]val} field of the {[cn]data} node.

      {[bm,E]} Calling {[cf]reverse} [codescape {[dc,4]}] plus modifying the {[cf]idx} and {[cf]val} fields further scrambles searches for {[cn]data}.

      {[bm,F]} {[cu]ListBench} eventually updates {[cn]retval} using its local {[cn]found} and {[cn]missed} tallies.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 92
      - mark 1 B 93
      - mark 1 C 97
      - mark 1 D 100
      - mark 1 E 101
      - mark 1 F 103
    focus: [1, 94]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]ListBench% &ndash; %[cf]run% Details (Sort Phase)}

      The second phase of {[cu]ListBench}.{[cf]run} continues updating {[cn]retval} by forming interim {[ct]sum_t} values at {[bm,C]} and {[bm,F]}.

      Here too, modifying the list using {[cf]remove} at {[bm,B]} further stresses the subsequent call to {[cf]find}.

      {[in]Calling %[cf]unremove% at %[bm,D]% restores the sidelined %[ct]Elem%.}

      This phase of {[cu]ListBench}.{[cf]run} also calls the {[cf]sort} helper twice:&nbsp;  with {[cf]valCompare}{[bm,A]} and with {[cf]idxCompare}{[bm,E]}.

      {[in] Look back and review codescape items %[dc,6]% and %[dc,7]%.}

      We'll soon dive into the details of {[cf]valCompare} (leaving the simpler {[cf]idxCompare} as an exercise for the reader).

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1, 169]
    text: |
      {[ht,$peek]&thinsp;%[cu]ListBench% &ndash; Helper Functions}

      The {[cf]remove} and {[cf]reverse} functions seen here typify the list-processing helpers used throughout {[cu]ListBench}.

      {[in]Review codescape item %[dc,3]% to fully understand the rather unusual side-effect of %[cf]remove%. }

      These samples show idiomatic use of the {[ct]$$} op&shy;erator &ndash; defining reference types and *de-referencing* their values.

      {[in]Depending upon context, the <b>EMS</b> %[ct]$$% operator trans&shy;lates to the %[cx]*%, %[cx]&amp;%, or %[cx]-&gt;% operator of <b>C/C++</b>.}

      Finally, skim through the {[cf]sort} function [code&shy;scape {[dc,5]}] which merges list fragments in a bottom-up manner.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 259
      - mark 1 B 267
      - mark 1 C 280
    focus: [1, 262]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]ListBench% &ndash; %[cn]val% %[ct]Comparator%}

      Recall that the {[cf]sort} routine used by {[cu]ListBench} receives a *callback* of type {[ct]Comparator} as an argument:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cf]idxCompare}, which orders based on {[ct]Data}.{[cn]idx} {[dc,6]}<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cf]valCompare}, which orders based on {[ct]Data}.{[cn]val} {[dc,7]}

      Both {[ct]Comparator} implementations return a signed value used by {[cf]sort} to sequence {[ct]Data} items {[cn]a} and {[cn]b}.

      The {[cf]valCalc} helper initially unpacks a *reference* to some {[ct]Data}.{[cn]val} at {[bm,A]}, which then feeds other numeric locals.

      The {[ck]switch} {[ck]case} at {[bm,B]} uses these locals to call {[cf]run} on a {[cn]Bench&lbrack;01&rbrack;} proxy and to bind its {[cf]kind} of checksum.

      {[bm,C]} updates the {[cn]FINAL} checksum before hash&shy;ing {[cn]ret} and modifying the original {[ct]Data}.{[cn]val} referenced via {[cn]pval}.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1, 137]
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;Extra Credit}

      {[bi,task_alt]} Find {[cu]ListBench} helpers inside {[cx]core_list_join.c}.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Review helper functions to sharpen your **EMS** skills.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Use the internal {[cf]pr} routine to inspect interim results.

  - cmds:
      - close 1
      - close 4
      - view 3
    text: |
      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      We've now explored the last (and most complex) of the three **CoreMark** algorithms.

      Though seemingly arbitrary, these algorithms do present a side-by-side comparison of legacy **C** code with **EMS**.

      {[in]The comparisons have hopefully sharpened your famili&shy;arity with the <b>EMS</b> language surface.}

      From here, we'll pivot to {[cu]SleepyRunnerP} &ndash; a minimal pro&shy;gram which quite literally turns **CoreMark** inside-out.

      {[hc]}
